{
  "version": "0.6.4",
  "notes": "0.6.3's rxbuf=4096 fix wasn't the whole story: the very next real run still cut a response short mid-chunk (714 of 1024 declared bytes, then no further chunks found at all -- the raw capture itself stopped early). Confirmed directly that the real JSON payload from GitHub contains zero \\r bytes, ruling out a coincidental '\\r\\nOK\\r\\n' match inside real content -- something in the AT protocol framing itself was producing a false-positive stop-token match in read_until(), cutting the capture off before the real end of the response. Fix: AT+HTTPREAD now uses a dedicated read_http_data() that never checks for '\\r\\nOK\\r\\n' as a stop condition at all -- it tracks how many bytes of actual '+HTTPREAD: DATA,<n>' chunk payload have arrived and only stops once that total reaches the length already declared by AT+HTTPACTION (or on \\r\\nERROR\\r\\n, or timeout). Verified with a mock UART feeding the response in small fragmented pieces (including splits mid-chunk-header) and content containing 'OK'/'ERROR'-like substrings -- reassembles correctly and doesn't stop early on either.",
